  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: a4da58d2dc2409c459ef20449bf92a90, type: 3}
  m_Name: Torsion
  m_EditorClassIdentifier:
  lessonId: civil-craft-torsion-lesson-v1
  lessonTitle: Torsion
  lessonImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: e2b0b0b1be918a04daa869ebe2205cdc, type: 3}
  popupDescription: "<b><#E09500>Torsion</color></b> is a twisting effect. A vehicle near one bridge edge can create it. Moving the same weight farther from the center of resistance can increase the twisting moment.\n\nConsider the load's position as well as its weight. This game test checks pulling and compression; twisting across the bridge width is not measured."
  almanacImage: {fileID: 0}
  lessonDescription: "<b><#E09500>Torsion</color></b> occurs when a moment acts around a structural member's longitudinal axis and tends to twist it. It differs from stretching, compression, or vertical bending.\n\n<b><#E09500>Off-center loading</color></b> can create torsion. A vehicle closer to one bridge edge may produce an uneven response across the width. For the same downward force, increasing its sideways distance from the structure's center of resistance increases the twisting moment. The actual response also depends on the bridge's shape, stiffness, connections, and supports.\n\nEngineers consider both the downward load and the twisting moment. Connected members across the deck help distribute loads and resist twisting. Excessive torsion can cause deformation, cracking, or failure. A centered load does not automatically remove every source of torsion.\n\nAt Clark's <b><#E09500>Offset Road</color></b>, the lane stays closer to one side to work around factory equipment. Consider why that position matters in a real bridge, and keep the roadway and supports connected throughout the SUV crossing.\n\n<b><#E09500>About this test</color></b>: this game test checks pulling and compression. Twisting across the bridge width is not measured. The offset lane illustrates the site constraint; passing this crossing does not prove that a real bridge can safely resist torsion."
